## 2. Capacity Estimation

State assumptions explicitly; the interviewer wants to hear your reasoning, not a memorized number.

### 2.1 Base assumptions
| Quantity | Assumption |
|---|---|
| Subscriber accounts | 300 million |
| Devices per account | 1.7 average (phone + TV + tablet mix) |
| Registered devices | **~500 million** |
| Avg. notifications per user per day | 2 (mix of recs, releases, downloads, account) |
| Daily notification volume | 300M × 2 = **~600M–1B/day** (using 800M as a working number) |

### 2.2 Throughput
- **Steady-state average**: 800,000,000 / 86,400 s ≈ **~9,300 notifications/sec**
- **Peak burst scenario** — a flagship season drops, targeting 80M eligible/opted-in devices, delivered over a 20-minute window:
  - 80,000,000 / 1,200 s ≈ **~67,000 notifications/sec peak**
- **Design implication**: peak-to-average ratio is roughly **7×**. The system must be provisioned and rate-limited for the peak, not the average — and more importantly, the *external* push providers (APNs/FCM) enforce their own throttling, so peak absorption is as much about **pacing** as raw throughput. See Deep Dive 1.

### 2.3 Storage
| Store | Row estimate | Row size | Total |
|---|---|---|---|
| Device registry | 500M rows | ~500 bytes (device_id, user_id, token, platform, locale, status, timestamps) | **~250 GB** |
| User preferences | 300M rows | ~300 bytes (flags map + quiet hours + locale) | **~90 GB** |
| Segment index (device_ids bucketed by category+region) | Derived, ~1–2× device registry due to multi-category membership | — | **~500 GB–1 TB** |
| Delivery log, 30-day retention | 800M/day × 30 = 24B rows × ~200 bytes | — | **~4.8 TB raw** (compresses well; time-series columnar storage recommended for the analytics copy) |

### 2.4 Bandwidth
- Avg. payload ~1 KB (title, body, deep link, image reference, metadata).
- Peak egress to provider gateways: 67,000/sec × 1 KB ≈ **~67 MB/s**, trivially within modern network capacity — **the bottleneck is not bandwidth, it's the providers' per-app rate limits and connection concurrency**, which is why the deep dive focuses there rather than on raw throughput provisioning.

### 2.5 Connections
- APNs: HTTP/2 over persistent TLS connections; a small number of multiplexed connections per dispatcher node can carry very high request concurrency (thousands of in-flight streams per connection) — so **connection count is not the constraint, provider-side rate limiting is**.
- FCM: supports multicast (batch) sends of up to 500 device tokens per API call — batching reduces the effective call count by ~500× versus per-device calls.

