## 5. High-Level Architecture

```
 Internal producers (catalog, recs, billing, live-event scheduler)
              │
      Notification / Campaign API  ◄── idempotency keys enforced here
              │
   Preference & Eligibility Filter  ── reads UserPreferences (opt-in, quiet hours, locale)
              │
       Fan-out / Targeting Service  ── reads SegmentIndex (bucketed), resolves device_id list
              │
        Kafka (partitioned by hash(device_id) or by region/shard)
              │
   ┌──────────┼───────────┬─────────────┐
Dispatcher   Dispatcher  Dispatcher   Dispatcher
 (APNs)       (FCM)      (Web Push)    (TV/other)
   │            │             │             │
   └── per-provider adaptive rate limiter (token bucket) ──┘
              │
     Retry w/ backoff  →  Dead Letter Queue (permanent failures, invalid tokens)
              │
      Delivery Log  →  Streaming analytics (funnel, CTR, provider error-rate dashboards)
```

**Why Kafka sits between fan-out and dispatch, specifically:** it decouples the *rate at which the fan-out service can resolve a segment* from the *rate at which providers can actually accept pushes*. Fan-out can burst-write 80M device_ids into Kafka quickly; dispatchers then pull at a sustainable, provider-safe rate. Kafka is the shock absorber for the entire burst problem.

**Storage choices and why:**
- **Cassandra/DynamoDB** for device registry & preferences: write-heavy, needs horizontal scale, and tunable/eventual consistency is an acceptable tradeoff (see Deep Dive 2).
- **Kafka** as the fan-out → dispatch buffer: high write throughput, natural backpressure when consumers slow down, replay capability if a dispatcher fleet needs to be restarted mid-campaign.
- **Redis** for hot-path preference/rate-limiter state: sub-millisecond reads for the transactional path and for live token-bucket state shared across dispatcher nodes.

