## Deep Dive 3: Reliability & Failure Scenarios

| Failure | Detection | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| APNs (or FCM) fully down | Circuit breaker sees error rate spike / connection failures | Adaptive token bucket for that provider drops to near-zero; messages queue in Kafka (bounded by TTL) rather than being dropped; **other providers' dispatchers are entirely separate consumer groups**, so Android/web delivery is unaffected — no shared fate. |
| Provider partially degraded (elevated latency, no errors) | Latency percentile tracking per provider, not just error rate | Token bucket shrinks proactively on latency SLO breach, not just on explicit 429/503 — catches "gray failure" before it becomes a hard failure. |
| Dispatcher fleet crash mid-campaign | Kafka consumer group rebalance; consumer lag alert | At-least-once semantics: in-flight messages are re-delivered to a healthy consumer; `notification_id` used as an idempotency key downstream so a user doesn't get a true duplicate even if the same message is dispatched twice. |
| Segment index CDC pipeline falls behind during a burst (preference writes spike alongside the campaign) | Consumer lag metric on the CDC topic | Fan-out reads accept slightly stale segment data (by design, per Deep Dive 2) rather than blocking; lag alert pages on-call if staleness exceeds a defined SLA (e.g., >5 min) rather than failing the campaign. |
| Malformed/broken campaign template | Canary phase (Deep Dive 1) catches this before full ramp | Canary error-rate threshold auto-pauses the campaign; on-call gets paged with the canary's specific error signature rather than discovering it after 80M sends. |
| Region-level outage (regional Kafka cluster or dispatcher fleet down) | Regional health check / control-plane heartbeat | Campaigns targeting the affected region pause automatically; other regions continue unaffected, since fan-out and dispatch are region-sharded, not globally coupled. |
| Duplicate delivery due to retry | N/A — expected under at-least-once | `notification_id` idempotency key deduplicates at the client SDK layer (or dispatcher checks DeliveryLog for an existing `sent` status before resending on retry). |

**Retry policy for transient provider errors (429/503/network timeout):** exponential backoff with jitter, capped attempts (e.g., 5), then routed to a dead-letter queue. DLQ entries are inspected by an automated job: token-invalid errors update the `Device` table (see 5.9); genuinely transient failures that exhausted retries get one final delayed retry after a longer cooldown; anything else is logged for manual/product review rather than retried indefinitely.

