## Deep Dive 4: Observability

**Delivery funnel, per campaign, updated near-real-time:** queued → sent → delivered → opened counts, streamed from the DeliveryLog into a time-series/analytics store (e.g., via the same Kafka backbone, consumed into a columnar analytics DB). This is what product teams look at to judge campaign effectiveness (CTR) and what on-call looks at to judge system health (delivered/sent ratio dropping is often the earliest signal of a provider problem).

**Key operational metrics:**
- Per-provider: send rate, 429/503 rate, p50/p99 latency.
- Kafka: consumer lag per shard/partition (leading indicator of dispatcher fleet falling behind).
- CDC pipeline: lag between a preference write and its reflection in SegmentIndex.
- DLQ depth and top error codes (token-invalid rate spiking can indicate an app-side bug pushing bad tokens).

**Alerting philosophy:** alert on *leading* indicators (Kafka lag, 429 rate trending up) rather than only *lagging* indicators (delivered/sent ratio, which by the time it's bad may mean a large fraction of a campaign already failed).

---

## Deep Dive 5: Security & Privacy

- **Push tokens are opaque, rotate periodically**, and are treated as sensitive — access to the Device table is scoped to the notification system's own services, not broadly readable.
- **Opt-out is enforced at the Preference & Eligibility Filter stage**, before fan-out — never as a post-hoc filter after dispatch, so an opt-out is honored even for in-flight campaign windows.
- **Regional data residency**: device/preference records for EU users can be pinned to EU-region storage if required, with the segment index and dispatch pipeline respecting the same regional boundary.
- **Right-to-be-forgotten**: deleting a user cascades — canonical `Device`/`UserPreferences` rows deleted, which flows through the same CDC pipeline used for token invalidation (5.9) to remove the user from all `SegmentIndex` buckets, rather than requiring a separate deletion pathway.

---

## 6. Trade-off Summary (for quick recall in the interview)

| Decision | Chosen approach | Rejected alternative | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Preference store consistency | Eventually consistent, LWW | Strongly consistent, quorum writes | A few seconds of staleness is an acceptable cost for a best-effort system; strong consistency adds latency/complexity the product doesn't need |
| Segment resolution | Precomputed, CDC-derived, bucketed index | Live query over canonical preference store | Avoids O(all users) scans per campaign; canonical store stays optimized for point reads |
| Burst absorption | Kafka buffer + adaptive per-provider token bucket + staggered shard dispatch | Direct synchronous fan-out to providers | Providers enforce hard rate limits; direct fan-out either gets throttled/rejected or risks provider penalties |
| Broad, non-personalized sends | Provider-native topic/multicast | Per-device dispatch for everything | Offloads fan-out to the provider entirely when personalization isn't needed |
| Delivery semantics | At-least-once + idempotency key | Exactly-once | Exactly-once is expensive to guarantee end-to-end across external providers you don't control; idempotency key gets the same user-facing result cheaper |
| Provider isolation | Fully separate consumer groups/dispatcher fleets per provider | Shared dispatch pool across providers | Prevents one provider's outage from degrading delivery on unaffected platforms |

